itle="Automatic transmission">automatic</a> with <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Manumatic" title="Manumatic">manumatic</a> shifting</td></tr><tr><th scope="row" class="infobox-label" style="padding-right:1em">Suspension</th><td class="infobox-data"><a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/4x4" title="4x4" class="mw-redirect">4x4</a>, <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Oshkosh_TAK-4_Independent_Suspension_System" title="Oshkosh TAK-4 Independent Suspension System">TAK-4 independent suspension</a></td></tr><tr><th scope="row" class="infobox-label" style="padding-right:1em">Ground<span typeof="mw:Entity"> </span>clearance</th><td class="infobox-data">375 mm<sup about="#mwt52" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-11" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-11"}}'><a href="#cite_note-11" id="mwNA"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwNQ"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwNg">[</span>11<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwNw">]</span></span></a></sup></td></tr><tr><th scope="row" class="infobox-label" style="padding-right:1em"><div style="line-height:1.25em">Operational<br/>range</div></th><td class="infobox-data">320 miles (510<span typeof="mw:Entity"> </span>km)</td></tr><tr><th scope="row" class="infobox-label" style="padding-right:1em"><span class="mw-empty-elt"><style data-mw-deduplicate="TemplateStyles:r1038841319" typeof="mw:Extension/templatestyles" about="#mwt55" data-mw='{"name":"templatestyles","attrs":{"src":"Template:Tooltip/styles.css"}}'>.mw-parser-output .tooltip-dotted{border-bottom:1px dotted;cursor:help}</style></span><span class="rt-commentedText tooltip  " title="Maximum speed of vehicle or missile" about="#mwt57" typeof="mw:ExpandedAttrs" data-mw='{"attribs":[[{"txt":"title"},{"html":"&lt;span typeof=\"mw:Nowiki\" id=\"mwOA\">Maximum speed of vehicle or missile&lt;/span>"}]]}'>Maximum speed</span></th><td class="infobox-data">65 miles per hour (105<span typeof="mw:Entity"> </span>km/h) (<a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Governor_(device)" title="Governor (device)">electronically limited</a>)</td></tr></tbody></table>

<p id="mwOQ">The <b id="mwOg">Oshkosh M-ATV</b> is a mine-resistant ambush protected (<a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/MRAP" title="MRAP" class="mw-redirect" id="mwOw">MRAP</a>) vehicle developed by the <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Oshkosh_Corporation" title="Oshkosh Corporation" id="mwPA">Oshkosh Corporation</a> for the MRAP All Terrain Vehicle (M-ATV) program. Intended to replace <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Humvee#Expanded_capacity" title="Humvee" id="mwPQ">M1114 HMMWVs</a> (Humvee), it is designed to provide the same levels of protection as the larger and heavier previous MRAPs, but with improved mobility.<sup about="#mwt59" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-Oshkosh_M-ATV_CD_5-2" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"Oshkosh_M-ATV_CD"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-Oshkosh_M-ATV_CD-5"}}'><a href="#cite_note-Oshkosh_M-ATV_CD-5" id="mwPg"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwPw"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwQA">[</span>5<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwQQ">]</span></span></a></sup></p>

<meta property="mw:PageProp/toc" id="mwQg" data-mw='{"autoGenerated":true}'/></section><section data-mw-section-id="1" id="mwQw" aria-labelledby="History"><div class="mw-heading mw-heading2"><h2 id="History">History</h2><span class="mw-editsection"><span class="mw-editsection-bracket">[</span><a href="/w/index.php?title=Oshkosh_M-ATV&amp;action=edit&amp;section=1" title="Edit section: History"><span>edit</span></a><span class="mw-editsection-bracket">]</span></span></div>

<section data-mw-section-id="2" id="mwRA" aria-labelledby="Requirements_and_selection"><div class="mw-heading mw-heading3"><h3 id="Requirements_and_selection">Requirements and selection</h3><span class="mw-editsection"><span class="mw-editsection-bracket">[</span><a href="/w/index.php?title=Oshkosh_M-ATV&amp;action=edit&amp;section=2" title="Edit section: Requirements and selection"><span>edit</span></a><span class="mw-editsection-bracket">]</span></span></div>
<figure class="mw-default-size mw-halign-left" typeof="mw:File/Thumb" id="mwRQ"><a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File:Fort_Irwin_National_Training_Center_-_M-ATV_-_3.jpg" class="mw-file-description" id="mwRg"><img resource="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File:Fort_Irwin_National_Training_Center_-_M-ATV_-_3.jpg" src="//thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2c/Fort_Irwin_National_Training_Center_-_M-ATV_-_3.jpg/250px-Fort_Irwin_National_Training_Center_-_M-ATV_-_3.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&amp;utm_campaign=parser&amp;utm_content=thumbnail" decoding="async" loading="lazy" srcset="//thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2c/Fort_Irwin_National_Training_Center_-_M-ATV_-_3.jpg/500px-Fort_Irwin_National_Training_Center_-_M-ATV_-_3.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&amp;utm_campaign=parser&amp;utm_content=thumbnail 2x" data-file-width="3146" data-file-height="1981" data-file-type="bitmap" height="120" width="190" class="mw-file-element mw-file-upright" style="--mw-file-upright: 0.75" id="mwRw"/></a><figcaption id="mwSA">M-ATV at the <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fort_Irwin_National_Training_Center" title="Fort Irwin National Training Center" id="mwSQ">Fort Irwin National Training Center</a> in November 2011</figcaption></figure>

<p id="mwSg">In the summer of 2008, the U.S. <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/United_States_Department_of_Defense" title="United States Department of Defense" id="mwSw">Department of Defense</a> (DoD) began to examine the possibility of developing and procuring a lighter-weight, all-terrain capable <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/MRAP" title="MRAP" class="mw-redirect" id="mwTA">MRAP</a> variant to address the poor roads and difficult terrain of <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Afghanistan" title="Afghanistan" id="mwTQ">Afghanistan</a>. Source selection activity considered responses from more than 20 companies to a <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Request_for_Information" title="Request for Information" class="mw-redirect" id="mwTg">Request for Information</a> (RFI)/market survey dated 21 August 2008 and in mid-November 2008 the U.S. government issued a pre-solicitation for an M-ATV. In early December 2008 the M-ATV formal <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Request_for_Proposals" title="Request for Proposals" class="mw-redirect" id="mwTw">Request for Proposals</a> (RFP) was issued. The original M-ATV program requirement was for between 372 and 10,000 vehicles, with the most probable production quantity stated as 2,080.<sup about="#mwt62" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-olive-drab.com_12-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"olive-drab.com"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-olive-drab.com-12"}}'><a href="#cite_note-olive-drab.com-12" id="mwUA"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwUQ"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwUg">[</span>12<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwUw">]</span></span></a></sup></p>

<p id="mwVA">In March 2009, it became known that two each of six different vehicle types (from five manufacturers) had been delivered to the <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/U.S._Army" title="U.S. Army" class="mw-redirect" id="mwVQ">U.S. Army</a> for two months of evaluation, at the conclusion of which up to five ID/IQ (<a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Indefinite_delivery/indefinite_quantity" title="Indefinite delivery/indefinite quantity" class="mw-redirect" id="mwVg">Indefinite Delivery/Indefinite Quantity</a>) contracts would be awarded.<sup about="#mwt66" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-Contractors_submit_M-ATV_prototypes_13-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"Contractors submit M-ATV prototypes"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-Contractors_submit_M-ATV_prototypes-13"}}'><a href="#cite_note-Contractors_submit_M-ATV_prototypes-13" id="mwVw"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwWA"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwWQ">[</span>13<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwWg">]</span></span></a></sup>  In addition to <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Oshkosh_Corporation" title="Oshkosh Corporation" id="mwWw">Oshkosh's</a> proposal, <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/BAE_Systems" title="BAE Systems" id="mwXA">BAE Systems</a> submitted two proposals: a <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Joint_Light_Tactical_Vehicle" title="Joint Light Tactical Vehicle" id="mwXQ">Joint Light Tactical Vehicle</a> (JLTV) derived design and an <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Family_of_Medium_Tactical_Vehicles" title="Family of Medium Tactical Vehicles" id="mwXg">FMTV</a>-based Caiman derivative. Force Dynamics (a Force Protection/<a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/General_Dynamics_Land_Systems" title="General Dynamics Land Systems" id="mwXw">General Dynamics Land Systems</a> (GDLS) joint venture) offered the <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cheetah_MMPV" title="Cheetah MMPV" id="mwYA">Cheetah</a>, GDLS-C (Canada) offered an <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/RG-31" title="RG-31" class="mw-redirect" id="mwYQ">RG-31</a> MRAP derivative, and <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Navistar" title="Navistar" class="mw-redirect" id="mwYg">Navistar</a> offered an <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/International_MXT-MV" title="International MXT-MV" id="mwYw">MXT</a>-based solution.<sup about="#mwt70" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-janes_Companies_gear_up_14-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"janes_Companies_gear_up"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-janes_Companies_gear_up-14"}}'><a href="#cite_note-janes_Companies_gear_up-14" id="mwZA"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwZQ"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwZg">[</span>14<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwZw">]</span></span></a></sup></p>

<figure class="mw-default-size mw-halign-left" typeof="mw:File/Thumb" id="mwaA"><a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File:USMC-110515-M-2678M-012.jpg" class="mw-file-description" id="mwaQ"><img resource="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File:USMC-110515-M-2678M-012.jpg" src="//thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e3/USMC-110515-M-2678M-012.jpg/250px-USMC-110515-M-2678M-012.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&amp;utm_campaign=parser&amp;utm_content=thumbnail" decoding="async" loading="lazy" srcset="//thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e3/USMC-110515-M-2678M-012.jpg/500px-USMC-110515-M-2678M-012.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&amp;utm_campaign=parser&amp;utm_content=thumbnail 2x" data-file-width="5616" data-file-height="3744" data-file-type="bitmap" height="127" width="190" class="mw-file-element mw-file-upright" style="--mw-file-upright: 0.75" id="mwag"/></a><figcaption id="mwaw">A Marine sniper with U.S. Marine Corps Forces, Special Operations Command, provides security from the back of an M-ATV during a medical engagement as part of a pre-deployment exercise</figcaption></figure>
<p id="mwbA">After GDLS-C's RG-31 was eliminated from the competition in May 2009, it was announced that the five remaining bidders had been awarded ID/IQ contracts, and were each to deliver three production-ready test vehicles for the next stage of the competition. At the completion of testing, the U.S. DoD stated that it planned to select a single M-ATV producer but could, at its discretion, place production orders with multiple producers as it had done with the initial MRAP procurement. On 30 June 2009, the M-ATV contract award was announced with a single ID/IQ contract award to Oshkosh.<sup about="#mwt74" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-MRAP_manufacturers_rev_up_15-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"MRAP_manufacturers_rev_up"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-MRAP_manufacturers_rev_up-15"}}'><a href="#cite_note-MRAP_manufacturers_rev_up-15" id="mwbQ"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwbg"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwbw">[</span>15<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwcA">]</span></span></a></sup><sup about="#mwt78" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-RG-31_down_16-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"RG-31_down"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-RG-31_down-16"}}'><a href="#cite_note-RG-31_down-16" id="mwcQ"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwcg"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwcw">[</span>16<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwdA">]</span></span></a></sup><sup about="#mwt82" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-Oshkosh_lands_M-ATV_contract_17-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"Oshkosh_lands_M-ATV_contract"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-Oshkosh_lands_M-ATV_contract-17"}}'><a href="#cite_note-Oshkosh_lands_M-ATV_contract-17" id="mwdQ"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwdg"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwdw">[</span>17<span class="cite-bracket" id="mweA">]</span></span></a></sup>  Brigadier General Michael Brogan, <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/United_States_Marine_Corps" title="United States Marine Corps" id="mweQ">United States Marine Corps</a> program officer for MRAP, stated that the Oshkosh M-ATV was chosen because it had the best survivability and Oshkosh had the best technical and manufacturing capabilities of all the competitors. The Oshkosh bid was also the second cheapest.<sup about="#mwt83" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-18" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-18"}}'><a href="#cite_note-18" id="mweg"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwew"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwfA">[</span>18<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwfQ">]</span></span></a></sup><sup about="#mwt85" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-19" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-19"}}'><a href="#cite_note-19" id="mwfg"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwfw"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwgA">[</span>19<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwgQ">]</span></span></a></sup><sup about="#mwt86" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-20" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-20"}}'><a href="#cite_note-20" id="mwgg"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwgw"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwhA">[</span>20<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwhQ">]</span></span></a></sup></p>

<p id="mwhg">The initial M-ATV delivery order was valued at over $1<span typeof="mw:Entity" id="mwhw"> </span>billion and included 2,244 M-ATVs. The overall M-ATV requirement had increased in early June from 2,080 to 5,244 M-ATVs, these split 2,598 (Army), 1,565 (Marines), 643 (<a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/U.S._Special_Operations_Command" title="U.S. Special Operations Command" class="mw-redirect" id="mwiA">U.S. Special Operations Command</a>), 280 (Air Force), 65 (Navy), and 93 for testing.<sup about="#mwt90" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-Pentagon_increases_M-ATV_requirement_21-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"Pentagon increases M-ATV requirement"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-Pentagon_increases_M-ATV_requirement-21"}}'><a href="#cite_note-Pentagon_increases_M-ATV_requirement-21" id="mwiQ"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwig"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwiw">[</span>21<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwjA">]</span></span></a></sup></p>

</section><section data-mw-section-id="3" id="mwjQ" aria-labelledby="Production_and_refurbishment"><div class="mw-heading mw-heading3"><h3 id="Production_and_refurbishment">Production and refurbishment</h3><span class="mw-editsection"><span class="mw-editsection-bracket">[</span><a href="/w/index.php?title=Oshkosh_M-ATV&amp;action=edit&amp;section=3" title="Edit section: Production and refurbishment"><span>edit</span></a><span class="mw-editsection-bracket">]</span></span></div>
<figure class="mw-default-size mw-halign-right" typeof="mw:File/Thumb" id="mwjg"><a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File:M-ATV,_Protecting_the_warfighter_170112-M-TR039-0007.jpg" class="mw-file-description" id="mwjw"><img resource="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File:M-ATV,_Protecting_the_warfighter_170112-M-TR039-0007.jpg" src="//thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/aa/M-ATV%2C_Protecting_the_warfighter_170112-M-TR039-0007.jpg/250px-M-ATV%2C_Protecting_the_warfighter_170112-M-TR039-0007.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&amp;utm_campaign=parser&amp;utm_content=thumbnail" decoding="async" loading="lazy" srcset="//thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/aa/M-ATV%2C_Protecting_the_warfighter_170112-M-TR039-0007.jpg/500px-M-ATV%2C_Protecting_the_warfighter_170112-M-TR039-0007.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&amp;utm_campaign=parser&amp;utm_content=thumbnail 2x" data-file-width="5760" data-file-height="3840" data-file-type="bitmap" height="167" width="250" class="mw-file-element" id="mwkA"/></a><figcaption id="mwkQ">M-ATV components being refurbished, 2017</figcaption></figure>

<p id="mwkg">In July 2009, the first 46 M-ATVs were delivered, and in November the 1,000th M-ATV was handed over. Oshkosh reached its contractual obligation to produce 1,000 M-ATVs per month ahead of schedule in December 2009, and by using its existing manufacturing facilities in <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Oshkosh,_WI" title="Oshkosh, WI" class="mw-redirect" id="mwkw">Oshkosh, WI</a> (50%), and making use of its recession-hit JLG <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Telescopic_handler" title="Telescopic handler" id="mwlA">telescopic handler</a> facility in McConnellsburg, PA (50%).<sup about="#mwt93" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-Oshkosh_follow-on_order_22-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"Oshkosh_follow-on_order"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-Oshkosh_follow-on_order-22"}}'><a href="#cite_note-Oshkosh_follow-on_order-22" id="mwlQ"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwlg"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwlw">[</span>22<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwmA">]</span></span></a></sup>  The first vehicles arrived in <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/War_in_Afghanistan_(2001–present)" title="War in Afghanistan (2001–present)" class="mw-redirect" id="mwmQ">Afghanistan</a> in October 2009 and were to be all delivered by March 2010.<sup about="#mwt95" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-23" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-23"}}'><a href="#cite_note-23" id="mwmg"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwmw"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwnA">[</span>23<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwnQ">]</span></span></a></sup></p>

<p id="mwng">In total 8,722 M-ATVs were delivered to the U.S. Army, U.S. Marine Corps, U.S. Air Force, and U.S. Special Operations Command (SOCOM). M-ATVs were delivered in two main variants. The base model is designated M1240 with the Objective Gunner Protection Kit [OGPK] manned turret; it is designated M1240A1 when fitted with the Underbody Improvement Kit (UIK). The second main variant is designated M1277 and is fitted with M153 <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/CROWS" title="CROWS" id="mwnw">CROWS</a> <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Remote_weapon_station" title="Remote weapon station" class="mw-redirect" id="mwoA">remote-controlled weapon station</a> (RCWS). Produced in smaller numbers, the SOCOM-specific variant is designated M1245; M1245A1 with UIK fitted.<sup about="#mwt98" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-Retasking_MRAP:_Life_after_Afghanistan_24-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"Retasking MRAP: Life after Afghanistan"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-Retasking_MRAP:_Life_after_Afghanistan-24"}}'><a href="#cite_note-Retasking_MRAP:_Life_after_Afghanistan-24" id="mwoQ"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwog"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwow">[</span>24<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwpA">]</span></span></a></sup></p>

<p id="mwpQ">As part of the overall divestiture of the MRAP fleet, the U.S. Government will keep about 80% (around 7,000) of the M-ATV fleet, 5,651 of these (inc. 250 for SOCOM) to be retained by the Army.<sup about="#mwt99" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-Retasking_MRAP:_Life_after_Afghanistan_24-1" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"Retasking MRAP: Life after Afghanistan"}}'><a href="#cite_note-Retasking_MRAP:_Life_after_Afghanistan-24" id="mwpg"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwpw"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwqA">[</span>24<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwqQ">]</span></span></a></sup> Work is currently underway at Oshkosh's <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wisconsin" title="Wisconsin" id="mwqg">Wisconsin</a> facility and the <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Red_River_Army_Depot" title="Red River Army Depot" id="mwqw">Red River Army Depot</a> to reset the around 7,000 M-ATVs retained to a common build standard. Oshkosh was awarded an initial 500-vehicle M-ATV Reset contract in August 2014. Three additional contract options for 100 vehicles each were awarded in December 2014. Total contract value is in excess of US$77<span typeof="mw:Entity" id="mwrA"> </span>million. Deliveries were scheduled to continue through September 2015.<sup about="#mwt100" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-Retasking_MRAP:_Life_after_Afghanistan_24-2" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"Retasking MRAP: Life after Afghanistan"}}'><a href="#cite_note-Retasking_MRAP:_Life_after_Afghanistan-24" id="mwrQ"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwrg"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwrw">[</span>24<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwsA">]</span></span></a></sup></p>

<p id="mwsQ">Reset work centers on returning vehicles to <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Low_rate_initial_production" title="Low rate initial production" id="mwsg">Low Rate Initial Production</a> (LRIP) 22 standard: essentially the build standard for the final M-ATV production batch. LRIP 22 includes upgrades such as the UIK and enhanced Automatic Fire Extinguishing System (AFES). Reset work also adds Engineering Change Proposals (ECPs) that include acoustic signature reduction (muffler), Modular Ammunition Restraint System (MARS) ammunition storage, and some Government Furnished Equipment (GFE) relocation.<sup about="#mwt101" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-Retasking_MRAP:_Life_after_Afghanistan_24-3" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"Retasking MRAP: Life after Afghanistan"}}'><a href="#cite_note-Retasking_MRAP:_Life_after_Afghanistan-24" id="mwsw"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwtA"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwtQ">[</span>24<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwtg">]</span></span></a></sup></p>

<p id="mwtw">On 28 May 2015, Oshkosh announced the U.S. Army had awarded it a contract modification for the reset of 360 additional M-ATVs. The modification includes options for the reset of up to 1,440 additional M-ATVs. Deliveries for this latest modification started October 2015. Oshkosh is on contract to reset a combined 1,160 M-ATVs with a total value of over $115<span typeof="mw:Entity" id="mwuA"> </span>million.<sup about="#mwt104" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-Additional_M-ATV_Reset_Contract_25-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"Additional M-ATV Reset Contract"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-Additional_M-ATV_Reset_Contract-25"}}'><a href="#cite_note-Additional_M-ATV_Reset_Contract-25" id="mwuQ"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwug"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwuw">[</span>25<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwvA">]</span></span></a></sup></p>

<p id="mwvQ">In January 2017, the US Marine Corps disclosed that they would upgrade and <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Preservation_and_restoration_of_automobiles" title="Preservation and restoration of automobiles" class="mw-redirect" id="mwvg">refurbish</a> around 80 M-ATVs over a five-month period, the work scheduled to take three to four weeks for each M-ATV and cost around $385,000 per vehicle, with both Marine and Air Force M-ATVs involved. The main difference between the two services M-ATVs is the armament; Air Force M-ATVs are fitted with a CROWS (Common Remotely Operated Weapon Station), while Marine M-ATVs are fitted with a manual OGPK (Objective Gunner Protection Kit) turret.<sup about="#mwt107" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-M-ATV:_Protecting_the_warfighter_26-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"M-ATV: Protecting the warfighter"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-M-ATV:_Protecting_the_warfighter-26"}}'><a href="#cite_note-M-ATV:_Protecting_the_warfighter-26" id="mwvw"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwwA"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwwQ">[</span>26<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwwg">]</span></span></a></sup></p>

</section><section data-mw-section-id="4" id="mwww" aria-labelledby="Developments"><div class="mw-heading mw-heading3"><h3 id="Developments">Developments</h3><span class="mw-editsection"><span class="mw-editsection-bracket">[</span><a href="/w/index.php?title=Oshkosh_M-ATV&amp;action=edit&amp;section=4" title="Edit section: Developments"><span>edit</span></a><span class="mw-editsection-bracket">]</span></span></div>
<p id="mwxA">At the <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Association_for_Unmanned_Vehicle_Systems_International" title="Association for Unmanned Vehicle Systems International" class="mw-redirect" id="mwxQ">Association for Unmanned Vehicle Systems International</a> (AUVSI) 2013 convention, Oshkosh announced the integration of the <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/TerraMax_(vehicle)" title="TerraMax (vehicle)" class="mw-redirect" id="mwxg">TerraMax</a> system onto the M-ATV to allow the type to be converted into an <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Unmanned_ground_vehicle" title="Unmanned ground vehicle" id="mwxw">unmanned ground vehicle</a>. The goal is to use the M-ATV as an unmanned platform for route clearance and counter <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Improvised_explosive_device" title="Improvised explosive device" id="mwyA">improvised explosive device</a> (IED) missions by engineers.<sup about="#mwt109" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-27" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-27"}}'><a href="#cite_note-27" id="mwyQ"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwyg"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwyw">[</span>27<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwzA">]</span></span></a></sup></p>

<figure class="mw-default-size" typeof="mw:File/Thumb" id="mwzQ"><a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File:M-ATV_in_Syria.jpg" class="mw-file-description" id="mwzg"><img resource="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File:M-ATV_in_Syria.jpg" src="//thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/40/M-ATV_in_Syria.jpg/250px-M-ATV_in_Syria.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&amp;utm_campaign=parser&amp;utm_content=thumbnail" decoding="async" loading="lazy" srcset="//thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/40/M-ATV_in_Syria.jpg/500px-M-ATV_in_Syria.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&amp;utm_campaign=parser&amp;utm_content=thumbnail 2x" data-file-width="4288" data-file-height="2848" data-file-type="bitmap" height="166" width="250" class="mw-file-element" id="mwzw"/></a><figcaption id="mw0A">An M-ATV used by U.S. troops near <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Manbij" title="Manbij" id="mw0Q">Manbij</a>, <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Syria" title="Syria" id="mw0g">Syria</a>, July 2018</figcaption></figure>
<p id="mw0w">In February 2015, Oshkosh Defense unveiled the M-ATV Extended Wheel Base Medical (EXM) variant at the <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/International_Defense_Exhibition?action=edit&amp;redlink=1" title="International Defense Exhibition (page does not exist)" class="new" typeof="mw:LocalizedAttrs" id="mw1A" data-mw-i18n='{"title":{"lang":"x-page","key":"red-link-title","params":["International Defense Exhibition"]}}'>International Defense Exhibition</a> (IDEX) in <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Abu_Dhabi" title="Abu Dhabi" id="mw1Q">Abu Dhabi</a>, <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/United_Arab_Emirates" title="United Arab Emirates" id="mw1g">United Arab Emirates</a>. This variant of the M-ATV has enough interior capacity to simultaneously transport two litter-bound patients, two ambulatory patients, a medic, commander and driver. The M-ATV EXM's customizable internal configuration also enables equipment to be accessed quickly by a centrally positioned medic.<sup about="#mwt112" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-Oshkosh_Debuts_M-ATV_Ambulance_IDEX_28-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"Oshkosh_Debuts_M-ATV_Ambulance_IDEX"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-Oshkosh_Debuts_M-ATV_Ambulance_IDEX-28"}}'><a href="#cite_note-Oshkosh_Debuts_M-ATV_Ambulance_IDEX-28" id="mw1w"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mw2A"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mw2Q">[</span>28<span class="cite-bracket" id="mw2g">]</span></span></a></sup></p>

<p id="mw2w">In February 2015, Oshkosh Defense and <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Alliant_Techsystems" title="Alliant Techsystems" id="mw3A">Alliant Techsystems</a> conducted a firing demonstration of the <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/M230_chain_gun#M230LF_and_MAWS" title="M230 chain gun" id="mw3Q">M230LF 30<span typeof="mw:Entity" id="mw3g"> </span>mm chain gun</a> on an M-ATV to demonstrate the viability and effectiveness of a medium caliber weapon system for light tactical vehicles. The live fire demonstration showcased improved accuracy in mobile engagements and improved lethality on the M-ATV using the gun, mounted on the R400S-Mk2, a 3-axis stabilized <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Remote_weapon_station" title="Remote weapon station" class="mw-redirect" id="mw3w">remote weapon station</a> weighing less than <span about="#mwt113" typeof="mw:Transclusion" id="mw4A" data-mw='{"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"convert","href":"./Template:Convert"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"400"},"2":{"wt":"kg"},"3":{"wt":"lb"},"abbr":{"wt":"on"}},"i":0}}]}'>400</span><span typeof="mw:Entity" about="#mwt113"> </span><span about="#mwt113">kg (880</span><span typeof="mw:Entity" about="#mwt113"> </span><span about="#mwt113">lb)</span>. The addition of the <span about="#mwt114" typeof="mw:Transclusion" id="mw4Q" data-mw='{"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"convert","href":"./Template:Convert"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"72.6"},"2":{"wt":"kg"},"3":{"wt":"lb"},"abbr":{"wt":"on"}},"i":0}}]}'>72.6</span><span typeof="mw:Entity" about="#mwt114"> </span><span about="#mwt114">kg (160</span><span typeof="mw:Entity" about="#mwt114"> </span><span about="#mwt114">lb)</span> M230LF stabilized on the RWS provides mobile precise lethality, usually reserved for heavier combat vehicles, with exceptional off-road mobility and MRAP levels of protection.<sup about="#mwt116" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-29" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-29"}}'><a href="#cite_note-29" id="mw4g"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mw4w"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mw5A">[</span>29<span class="cite-bracket" id="mw5Q">]</span></span></a></sup></p>

<figure class="mw-default-size" typeof="mw:File/Thumb" id="mw5g"><a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File:Snow_Storm_DVIDS227343.jpg" class="mw-file-description" id="mw5w"><img resource="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File:Snow_Storm_DVIDS227343.jpg" src="//thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/ea/Snow_Storm_DVIDS227343.jpg/250px-Snow_Storm_DVIDS227343.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&amp;utm_campaign=parser&amp;utm_content=thumbnail" decoding="async" loading="lazy" srcset="//thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/ea/Snow_Storm_DVIDS227343.jpg/500px-Snow_Storm_DVIDS227343.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&amp;utm_campaign=parser&amp;utm_content=thumbnail 2x" data-file-width="5548" data-file-height="3700" data-file-type="bitmap" height="167" width="250" class="mw-file-element" id="mw6A"/></a><figcaption id="mw6Q">An M-ATV being delivered to the U.S. Forward Operating Base Airborne in <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Maidan_Wardak_Province" title="Maidan Wardak Province" id="mw6g">Wardak</a>, <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Afghanistan" title="Afghanistan" id="mw6w">Afghanistan</a></figcaption></figure>
<p id="mw7A">The M-ATV proved to be more survivable than the Humvee and was lighter than other MRAP versions, but to further enhance survivability and mobility for troops, the U.S. military undertook the <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Joint_Light_Tactical_Vehicle" title="Joint Light Tactical Vehicle" id="mw7Q">Joint Light Tactical Vehicle</a> (JLTV) program to obtain a vehicle combining light weight, mobility, and protection. In August 2015, Oshkosh was awarded the contract for their Light Combat Tactical All-Terrain Vehicle (<a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Oshkosh_L-ATV" title="Oshkosh L-ATV" class="mw-redirect" id="mw7g">L-ATV</a>), which took design lessons from fielding the M-ATV and incorporated them into a truck at two-thirds the weight and with faster off-road speeds.<sup about="#mwt119" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-JLTV_30-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"JLTV"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-JLTV-30"}}'><a href="#cite_note-JLTV-30" id="mw7w"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mw8A"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mw8Q">[</span>30<span class="cite-bracket" id="mw8g">]</span></span></a></sup></p>

<p id="mw8w">By 2018, the Marine Corps was deploying a Counter-<a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Unmanned_Aerial_System" title="Unmanned Aerial System" class="mw-redirect" id="mw9A">Unmanned Aerial System</a> (C-UAS) that can be mounted onto an M-ATV. The Ground-Based Air Defense (GBAD) Counter-UAS system consists of the RPS-42 S-band radar, the Modi electronic warfare system, visual sensors and <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Raytheon_Coyote" title="Raytheon Coyote" id="mw9Q">Raytheon Coyote</a> anti-drone UAV to detect, track and destroy hostile drones.<sup about="#mwt120" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-31" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-31"}}'><a href="#cite_note-31" id="mw9g"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mw9w"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mw-A">[</span>31<span class="cite-bracket" id="mw-Q">]</span></span></a></sup></p>

</section></section><section data-mw-section-id="5" id="mw-g" aria-labelledby="Design"><div class="mw-heading mw-heading2"><h2 id="Design">Design</h2><span class="mw-editsection"><span class="mw-editsection-bracket">[</span><a href="/w/index.php?title=Oshkosh_M-ATV&amp;action=edit&amp;section=5" title="Edit section: Design"><span>edit</span></a><span class="mw-editsection-bracket">]</span></span></div>
<figure class="mw-default-size mw-halign-left" typeof="mw:File/Thumb" id="mw-w"><a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File:Mine_resistant_ambush_protected_ATV.jpg" class="mw-file-description" id="mw_A"><img resource="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File:Mine_resistant_ambush_protected_ATV.jpg" src="//thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/cc/Mine_resistant_ambush_protected_ATV.jpg/250px-Mine_resistant_ambush_protected_ATV.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&amp;utm_campaign=parser&amp;utm_content=thumbnail" decoding="async" loading="lazy" srcset="//thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/cc/Mine_resistant_ambush_protected_ATV.jpg/500px-Mine_resistant_ambush_protected_ATV.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&amp;utm_campaign=parser&amp;utm_content=thumbnail 2x" data-file-width="2400" data-file-height="1685" data-file-type="bitmap" height="133" width="190" class="mw-file-element mw-file-upright" style="--mw-file-upright: 0.75" id="mw_Q"/></a><figcaption id="mw_g">An early model M-ATV in September 2009, equipped with a manned turret</figcaption></figure>

<p id="mw_w">The M-ATV combines a <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Plasan" title="Plasan" id="mwAQA">Plasan</a> designed armored hull developed for the <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Northrop_Grumman" title="Northrop Grumman" id="mwAQE">Northrop Grumman</a>/Oshkosh JLTV Technology Development (TD) phase proposal<sup about="#mwt121" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-32" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-32"}}'><a href="#cite_note-32" id="mwAQI"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwAQM"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAQQ">[</span>32<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAQU">]</span></span></a></sup><sup about="#mwt122" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-JLTV_30-1" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"JLTV"}}'><a href="#cite_note-JLTV-30" id="mwAQY"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwAQc"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAQg">[</span>30<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAQk">]</span></span></a></sup> with some elements of the <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Medium_Tactical_Vehicle_Replacement" title="Medium Tactical Vehicle Replacement" id="mwAQo">Medium Tactical Vehicle Replacement</a> (MTVR) chassis and Oshkosh's <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Oshkosh_TAK-4_Independent_Suspension_System" title="Oshkosh TAK-4 Independent Suspension System" id="mwAQs">TAK-4</a> suspension system. TAK-4 suspension is <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Coil_spring" title="Coil spring" id="mwAQw">coil sprung</a> and fully <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Independent_suspension" title="Independent suspension" id="mwAQ0">independent</a>, and offers 16 inches of wheel travel.</p>

<p id="mwAQ4">For survivability, and in addition to the V-shaped hull which is optimized for IED protection, other aids include the ability to take a <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/7.62×51mm_NATO" title="7.62×51mm NATO" id="mwAQ8">7.62<span typeof="mw:Entity" id="mwARA"> </span>mm</a> round to the engine oil/coolant/hydraulic system and continue to drive for at least one kilometer. The engine compartment is also protected with the Stat-X engine fire suppression system.<sup about="#mwt125" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-Stat-X_Our_History_33-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"Stat-X Our History"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-Stat-X_Our_History-33"}}'><a href="#cite_note-Stat-X_Our_History-33" id="mwARE"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwARI"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwARM">[</span>33<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwARQ">]</span></span></a></sup> A central tire inflation system (CTIS) and <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Run-flat_tire" title="Run-flat tire" id="mwARU">run-flat inserts</a> allow the M-ATV to travel at least 30 miles at 30<span typeof="mw:Entity" id="mwARY"> </span>mph even if two tires lose pressure. The M-ATV also features a <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Traction_control_system" title="Traction control system" id="mwARc">traction control</a> system and <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Anti-lock_braking_system" title="Anti-lock braking system" id="mwARg">anti-lock brakes</a>. </p>

<p id="mwARk">Armament is roof-mounted and can be either manually with <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gun_shield" title="Gun shield" id="mwARo">gun shield</a> or remotely operated. Manual options include a <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/M240_machine_gun" title="M240 machine gun" id="mwARs">M240 machine gun</a>, a <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mk_19_grenade_launcher" title="Mk 19 grenade launcher" id="mwARw">Mk 19 grenade launcher</a>, an <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/M2_Browning_machine_gun" title="M2 Browning machine gun" class="mw-redirect" id="mwAR0">M2 Browning machine gun</a>, a <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/MILAN" title="MILAN" id="mwAR4">MILAN</a> <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Anti-tank_guided_missile" title="Anti-tank guided missile" id="mwAR8">anti-tank guided missile</a>, or a <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/BGM-71_TOW" title="BGM-71 TOW" id="mwASA">BGM-71 TOW</a> anti-tank guided missile launcher. Remote option is usually the CROWS (Common Remotely Operated Weapon Station), however as previously mentioned, Oshkosh has also fitted for demonstration purposes the R400S-Mk2, a 3-axis stabilized remote weapon station when has the <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/M230_chain_gun" title="M230 chain gun" id="mwASE">M230LF</a> <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chain_gun" title="Chain gun" id="mwASI">chain</a> <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Autocannon" title="Autocannon" id="mwASM">autocannon</a> mounted on.</p>

<p id="mwASQ">Other M-ATV features include a <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/HVAC" title="HVAC" class="mw-redirect" id="mwASU">HVAC</a> system and power outlets for charging portable electronic devices.<sup about="#mwt126" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-Oshkosh_M-ATV_CD_5-3" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"Oshkosh_M-ATV_CD"}}'><a href="#cite_note-Oshkosh_M-ATV_CD-5" id="mwASY"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwASc"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwASg">[</span>5<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwASk">]</span></span></a></sup> The M-ATV is also unique among MRAP-type designs in that it uses <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Suicide_door" title="Suicide door" id="mwASo">suicide-type</a> rear doors.<sup about="#mwt127" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-Pentagon_increases_M-ATV_requirement_21-1" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"Pentagon increases M-ATV requirement"}}'><a href="#cite_note-Pentagon_increases_M-ATV_requirement-21" id="mwASs"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwASw"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAS0">[</span>21<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAS4">]</span></span></a></sup></p>

</section><section data-mw-section-id="6" id="mwAS8" aria-labelledby="Exports"><div class="mw-heading mw-heading2"><h2 id="Exports">Exports</h2><span class="mw-editsection"><span class="mw-editsection-bracket">[</span><a href="/w/index.php?title=Oshkosh_M-ATV&amp;action=edit&amp;section=6" title="Edit section: Exports"><span>edit</span></a><span class="mw-editsection-bracket">]</span></span></div>
<p id="mwATA">The <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/United_Arab_Emirates_Army" title="United Arab Emirates Army" id="mwATE">United Arab Emirates Army</a> initially ordered 55 M-ATVs through an <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Foreign_military_sales" title="Foreign military sales" class="mw-redirect" id="mwATI">FMS sale</a> in 2011. The UAE ordered another 750 M-ATVs direct from Oshkosh in July 2012. These are to provide greater off-road mobility and crew protection for regional security and peace-keeping operations; users include the elite Presidential Guard. Deliveries were completed in August 2013.<sup about="#mwt128" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-34" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-34"}}'><a href="#cite_note-34" id="mwATM"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwATQ"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwATU">[</span>34<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwATY">]</span></span></a></sup><sup about="#mwt130" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-35" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-35"}}'><a href="#cite_note-35" id="mwATc"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwATg"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwATk">[</span>35<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwATo">]</span></span></a></sup><sup about="#mwt133" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-SOFEX_2014_36-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"SOFEX_2014"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-SOFEX_2014-36"}}'><a href="#cite_note-SOFEX_2014-36" id="mwATs"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwATw"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAT0">[</span>36<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAT4">]</span></span></a></sup>  In September 2014, the UAE requested another 44 M-ATVs from U.S. surplus stocks.<sup about="#mwt134" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-37" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-37"}}'><a href="#cite_note-37" id="mwAT8"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwAUA"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAUE">[</span>37<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAUI">]</span></span></a></sup></p>

<p id="mwAUM">In September 2013, the <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Saudi_Arabian_Army" title="Saudi Arabian Army" id="mwAUQ">Saudi Arabian Army</a> began negotiations for an order for an undisclosed number of M-ATVs.<sup about="#mwt135" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-38" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-38"}}'><a href="#cite_note-38" id="mwAUU"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwAUY"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAUc">[</span>38<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAUg">]</span></span></a></sup> <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Saudi_Arabia" title="Saudi Arabia" id="mwAUk">Saudi Arabia</a> received an estimated 450 M-ATVs including some extended wheelbase variants.<sup about="#mwt136" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-M-ATV-ewb_4-1" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"M-ATV-ewb"}}'><a href="#cite_note-M-ATV-ewb-4" id="mwAUo"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwAUs"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAUw">[</span>4<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAU0">]</span></span></a></sup></p>

<figure class="mw-default-size" typeof="mw:File/Thumb" id="mwAU4"><a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File:Uvježbavanje_mehaniziranih_ešalona_Hrvatske_vojske_za_svečani_vojni_mimohod_povodom_30._obljetnice_VRO_Oluja.jpg" class="mw-file-description" id="mwAU8"><img resource="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File:Uvježbavanje_mehaniziranih_ešalona_Hrvatske_vojske_za_svečani_vojni_mimohod_povodom_30._obljetnice_VRO_Oluja.jpg" src="//thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/96/Uvje%C5%BEbavanje_mehaniziranih_e%C5%A1alona_Hrvatske_vojske_za_sve%C4%8Dani_vojni_mimohod_povodom_30._obljetnice_VRO_Oluja.jpg/250px-Uvje%C5%BEbavanje_mehaniziranih_e%C5%A1alona_Hrvatske_vojske_za_sve%C4%8Dani_vojni_mimohod_povodom_30._obljetnice_VRO_Oluja.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&amp;utm_campaign=parser&amp;utm_content=thumbnail" decoding="async" loading="lazy" srcset="//thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/96/Uvje%C5%BEbavanje_mehaniziranih_e%C5%A1alona_Hrvatske_vojske_za_sve%C4%8Dani_vojni_mimohod_povodom_30._obljetnice_VRO_Oluja.jpg/500px-Uvje%C5%BEbavanje_mehaniziranih_e%C5%A1alona_Hrvatske_vojske_za_sve%C4%8Dani_vojni_mimohod_povodom_30._obljetnice_VRO_Oluja.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&amp;utm_campaign=parser&amp;utm_content=thumbnail 2x" data-file-width="4964" data-file-height="3304" data-file-type="bitmap" height="166" width="250" class="mw-file-element" id="mwAVA"/></a><figcaption id="mwAVE"><a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Croatian_Army" title="Croatian Army" id="mwAVI">Croatian Army</a> M-ATV</figcaption></figure>
<p id="mwAVM">On 7 April 2014, the U.S. government donated 162 M-ATVs to the <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Croatian_Army" title="Croatian Army" id="mwAVQ">Croatian Army</a> for use in small-scale combat operations in urban and restricted environments.<sup about="#mwt138" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-Croatia_donation_39-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"Croatia donation"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-Croatia_donation-39"}}'><a href="#cite_note-Croatia_donation-39" id="mwAVU"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwAVY"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAVc">[</span>39<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAVg">]</span></span></a></sup> Fifteen M-ATVs are going to the Croatian Special Forces Command (SFCOM), five will be with the Support Command (SCOM), two with the Military Police Regiment, and 78 entered service with the Croatian Army in 2015, with a further 62 to follow in 2016 for the 1st Battalion of the Motorized Guards Brigade in Gospic.<sup about="#mwt141" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-Croatia_new_AFV_40-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"Croatia_new_AFV"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-Croatia_new_AFV-40"}}'><a href="#cite_note-Croatia_new_AFV-40" id="mwAVk"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwAVo"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAVs">[</span>40<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAVw">]</span></span></a></sup></p>

<p id="mwAV0">In January 2015, it was reported that the U.S. was to donate 308 MRAP vehicles to <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Uzbekistan" title="Uzbekistan" id="mwAV4">Uzbekistan</a> under the Excess Defense Articles program. Requested totals include 159 M-ATVs with UIK, plus 50 Maxxpro Plus, 20 MaxxPro recovery, 50 BAE RG-33L CAT II and 70 Cougar CAT 1 (W/ISS (65); W/O ISS 5) vehicles.<sup about="#mwt144" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-MRAPs_to_Uzbekistan_41-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"MRAPs_to_Uzbekistan"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-MRAPs_to_Uzbekistan-41"}}'><a href="#cite_note-MRAPs_to_Uzbekistan-41" id="mwAV8"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwAWA"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAWE">[</span>41<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAWI">]</span></span></a></sup><sup about="#mwt148" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-Excess_Defense_Articles_(EDA)_42-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"Excess Defense Articles (EDA)"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-Excess_Defense_Articles_(EDA)-42"}}'><a href="#cite_note-Excess_Defense_Articles_(EDA)-42" id="mwAWM"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwAWQ"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAWU">[</span>42<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAWY">]</span></span></a></sup></p>

<p id="mwAWc">In February 2015, it was disclosed that the U.S. was providing 20 M-ATVs to <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/African_Union" title="African Union" id="mwAWg">African Union</a> (AU) peacekeepers in <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Somalia" title="Somalia" id="mwAWk">Somalia</a>. These M-ATVs will replace older 1980s-vintage <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Casspir" title="Casspir" id="mwAWo">Casspir</a> vehicles.<sup about="#mwt151" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-MRAPs_for_Somalia_43-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"MRAPs for Somalia"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-MRAPs_for_Somalia-43"}}'><a href="#cite_note-MRAPs_for_Somalia-43" id="mwAWs"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwAWw"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAW0">[</span>43<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAW4">]</span></span></a></sup></p>

<figure class="mw-default-size mw-halign-right" typeof="mw:File/Thumb" id="mwAW8"><a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File:Polish_Oshkosh_M-ATV_M1240A1_Ostrava_2021.jpg" class="mw-file-description" id="mwAXA"><img resource="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File:Polish_Oshkosh_M-ATV_M1240A1_Ostrava_2021.jpg" src="//thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/78/Polish_Oshkosh_M-ATV_M1240A1_Ostrava_2021.jpg/250px-Polish_Oshkosh_M-ATV_M1240A1_Ostrava_2021.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&amp;utm_campaign=parser&amp;utm_content=thumbnail" decoding="async" loading="lazy" srcset="//thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/78/Polish_Oshkosh_M-ATV_M1240A1_Ostrava_2021.jpg/500px-Polish_Oshkosh_M-ATV_M1240A1_Ostrava_2021.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&amp;utm_campaign=parser&amp;utm_content=thumbnail 2x" data-file-width="1200" data-file-height="800" data-file-type="bitmap" height="167" width="250" class="mw-file-element" id="mwAXE"/></a><figcaption id="mwAXI"><a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Polish_Special_Forces" title="Polish Special Forces" id="mwAXM">Polish Special Forces</a> M-ATV</figcaption></figure>
<p id="mwAXQ">On 25 February 2015, Polish special forces received 45 M-ATVs. The handover ceremony took place in <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kraków" title="Kraków" id="mwAXU">Kraków</a>, Poland and the US Ambassador in Poland, <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Stephen_D._Mull" title="Stephen D. Mull" id="mwAXY">Stephen D. Mull</a>, participated in the event. Delivery of the MRAP vehicles was carried out within the framework of the Excess Defense Articles program, the standard way that the U.S. military gives surplus equipment to allies.<sup about="#mwt154" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-Oshkosh_Defense_Debuts_M-ATVs_to_Poland_44-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"Oshkosh Defense Debuts M-ATVs to Poland"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-Oshkosh_Defense_Debuts_M-ATVs_to_Poland-44"}}'><a href="#cite_note-Oshkosh_Defense_Debuts_M-ATVs_to_Poland-44" id="mwAXc"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwAXg"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAXk">[</span>44<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAXo">]</span></span></a></sup></p>

<p id="mwAXs">In June 2016, photographs released by the <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Iraq" title="Iraq" id="mwAXw">Iraqi</a> Ministry of Defence showed M-ATVs with <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Iraqi_Special_Operations_Forces" title="Iraqi Special Operations Forces" class="mw-redirect" id="mwAX0">Iraqi Special Operations Forces</a> (ISOF) (also known as the Counter-Terrorism Service (CTS)) units advancing northwards for the <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Battle_of_Mosul_(2016–2017)" title="Battle of Mosul (2016–2017)" id="mwAX4">operation to retake the Islamic State-held city of Mosul</a>.<sup about="#mwt157" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-Iraqi_special_forces_new_vehicles_45-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"Iraqi special forces new vehicles"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-Iraqi_special_forces_new_vehicles-45"}}'><a href="#cite_note-Iraqi_special_forces_new_vehicles-45" id="mwAX8"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwAYA"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAYE">[</span>45<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAYI">]</span></span></a></sup></p>

</section><section data-mw-section-id="7" id="mwAYM" aria-labelledby="Variants"><div class="mw-heading mw-heading2"><h2 id="Variants">Variants</h2><span class="mw-editsection"><span class="mw-editsection-bracket">[</span><a href="/w/index.php?title=Oshkosh_M-ATV&amp;action=edit&amp;section=7" title="Edit section: Variants"><span>edit</span></a><span class="mw-editsection-bracket">]</span></span></div>
<figure class="mw-default-size mw-halign-right" typeof="mw:File/Thumb" id="mwAYQ"><a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File:M-ATV_AUSA.jpg" class="mw-file-description" id="mwAYU"><img resource="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File:M-ATV_AUSA.jpg" src="//thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d3/M-ATV_AUSA.jpg/250px-M-ATV_AUSA.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&amp;utm_campaign=parser&amp;utm_content=thumbnail" decoding="async" loading="lazy" srcset="//thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d3/M-ATV_AUSA.jpg/500px-M-ATV_AUSA.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&amp;utm_campaign=parser&amp;utm_content=thumbnail 2x" data-file-width="1200" data-file-height="803" data-file-type="bitmap" height="167" width="250" class="mw-file-element" id="mwAYY"/></a><figcaption id="mwAYc"><a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Association_of_the_United_States_Army" title="Association of the United States Army" id="mwAYg">AUSA</a> expo. in 2016 for first public showing of M-ATV in Assault configuration.</figcaption></figure>

<p id="mwAYk">Oshkosh formally announced the addition of designated variants to the M-ATV family in April 2014.<sup about="#mwt160" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-Oshkosh_Defense_Introduces_New_M-ATV_Variants_at_SOFEX_2014_46-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"Oshkosh Defense Introduces New M-ATV Variants at SOFEX 2014"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-Oshkosh_Defense_Introduces_New_M-ATV_Variants_at_SOFEX_2014-46"}}'><a href="#cite_note-Oshkosh_Defense_Introduces_New_M-ATV_Variants_at_SOFEX_2014-46" id="mwAYo"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwAYs"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAYw">[</span>46<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAY0">]</span></span></a></sup> The range has evolved since then, the current five variants announced in May 2016.<sup about="#mwt163" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-Oshkosh_Defense_Offers_Five_M-ATV_Variants_to_Meet_a_Full_Range_of_Mission_Requirements_47-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"Oshkosh Defense Offers Five M-ATV Variants to Meet a Full Range of Mission Requirements"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-Oshkosh_Defense_Offers_Five_M-ATV_Variants_to_Meet_a_Full_Range_of_Mission_Requirements-47"}}'><a href="#cite_note-Oshkosh_Defense_Offers_Five_M-ATV_Variants_to_Meet_a_Full_Range_of_Mission_Requirements-47" id="mwAY4"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwAY8"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAZA">[</span>47<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAZE">]</span></span></a></sup></p>

<p id="mwAZI">The five current variants are:<sup about="#mwt166" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-M-ATV_48-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"M-ATV"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-M-ATV-48"}}'><a href="#cite_note-M-ATV-48" id="mwAZM"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwAZQ"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAZU">[</span>48<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAZY">]</span></span></a></sup></p>
<ul id="mwAZc"><li id="mwAZg"><b id="mwAZk">M-ATV <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Special_forces" title="Special forces" id="mwAZo">Special Forces</a></b> – 5-seat with protected cargo area. Curb Weight: 31,467 lb (14,273 kg); payload: 5,500 lb (2,495 kg).<sup about="#mwt169" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-49" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-49"}}'><a href="#cite_note-49" id="mwAZs"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwAZw"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAZ0">[</span>49<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAZ4">]</span></span></a></sup></li>
<li id="mwAZ8"><b id="mwAaA">M-ATV Assault</b> – modular seating for up to 11. Curb Weight: 35,450 lb (16,080 kg); payload: 4,400 lb (1,996 kg). Extended wheelbase.<sup about="#mwt172" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-50" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-50"}}'><a href="#cite_note-50" id="mwAaE"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwAaI"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAaM">[</span>50<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAaQ">]</span></span></a></sup></li>
<li id="mwAaU"><b id="mwAaY">M-ATV Engineer</b> – modular seating for 5 to 11. Curb Weight: 35,225 lb (15,978 kg); Payload: 4,400 lb (1,996 kg). Mine roller ready. Extended wheelbase.<sup about="#mwt175" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-51" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-51"}}'><a href="#cite_note-51" id="mwAac"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwAag"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAak">[</span>51<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAao">]</span></span></a></sup></li>
<li id="mwAas"><b id="mwAaw">M-ATV Command</b> – 5-seat. Curb Weight: 35,128 lb (15,934 kg); payload: 4,400 lb (1,996 kg). Extended wheelbase.<sup about="#mwt178" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-52" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-52"}}'><a href="#cite_note-52" id="mwAa0"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwAa4"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAa8">[</span>52<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAbA">]</span></span></a></sup></li>
<li id="mwAbE"><b id="mwAbI">M-ATV Utility</b> – 5-seat. Curb weight w/fuel: 29,344 lb (13,310 kg); payload: 7,000 lb (3,181 kg). Flatbed platform with ISO-lock corner fixings. Extended wheelbase.<sup about="#mwt181" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-53" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-53"}}'><a href="#cite_note-53" id="mwAbM"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwAbQ"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAbU">[</span>53<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAbY">]</span></span></a></sup></li></ul>
<figure class="mw-default-size" typeof="mw:File/Thumb" id="mwAbc"><a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File:M-LIDS.jpg" class="mw-file-description" id="mwAbg"><img resource="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File:M-LIDS.jpg" src="//thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/23/M-LIDS.jpg/250px-M-LIDS.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&amp;utm_campaign=parser&amp;utm_content=thumbnail" decoding="async" loading="lazy" srcset="//thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/23/M-LIDS.jpg/500px-M-LIDS.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&amp;utm_campaign=parser&amp;utm_content=thumbnail 2x" data-file-width="6673" data-file-height="4449" data-file-type="bitmap" height="167" width="250" class="mw-file-element" id="mwAbk"/></a><figcaption id="mwAbo">M-LIDS</figcaption></figure>
<ul id="mwAbs"><li id="mwAbw"><b id="mwAb0">M-ATV <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mobile-Low,_Slow,_Small_Unmanned_Aircraft_Integrated_Defeat_System" title="Mobile-Low, Slow, Small Unmanned Aircraft Integrated Defeat System" id="mwAb4">M-LIDS</a></b> – a counter-drone system developed by <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Leonardo_DRS" title="Leonardo DRS" id="mwAb8">Leonardo DRS</a>.<sup about="#mwt184" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-54" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-54"}}'><a href="#cite_note-54" id="mwAcA"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwAcE"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAcI">[</span>54<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAcM">]</span></span></a></sup>
<ul id="mwAcQ"><li id="mwAcU"><a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/M230_chain_gun" title="M230 chain gun" id="mwAcY">M230 chain gun</a> 30mm</li>
<li id="mwAcc"><a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Raytheon_Coyote" title="Raytheon Coyote" id="mwAcg">Coyote Block 2 Interceptor</a><sup about="#mwt187" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-55" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-55"}}'><a href="#cite_note-55" id="mwAck"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwAco"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAcs">[</span>55<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAcw">]</span></span></a></sup></li>
<li id="mwAc0">AN/TPQ-50 KuRFS multi-mission radar</li>
<li id="mwAc4">electro-optical infrared camera</li>
<li id="mwAc8">direction-finding sensors</li>
<li id="mwAdA">counter-drone electronic warfare system;</li>
<li id="mwAdE">forward area air defence command and control (FAAD C2) with mesh-net internet protocol radios</li></ul></li></ul>

<section data-mw-section-id="8" id="mwAdI" aria-labelledby="6×6_technology_demonstrator"><div class="mw-heading mw-heading3"><h3 id="6×6_technology_demonstrator"><span id="6.C3.976_technology_demonstrator" typeof="mw:FallbackId"></span>6×6 technology demonstrator</h3><span class="mw-editsection"><span class="mw-editsection-bracket">[</span><a href="/w/index.php?title=Oshkosh_M-ATV&amp;action=edit&amp;section=8" title="Edit section: 6×6 technology demonstrator"><span>edit</span></a><span class="mw-editsection-bracket">]</span></span></div>
<figure class="mw-default-size mw-halign-right" typeof="mw:File/Thumb" id="mwAdM"><a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File:Oshkosh_Defense_(6x6)_M-ATV_at_AUSA_2015.JPG" class="mw-file-description" id="mwAdQ"><img resource="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File:Oshkosh_Defense_(6x6)_M-ATV_at_AUSA_2015.JPG" src="//thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9d/Oshkosh_Defense_%286x6%29_M-ATV_at_AUSA_2015.JPG/250px-Oshkosh_Defense_%286x6%29_M-ATV_at_AUSA_2015.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&amp;utm_campaign=parser&amp;utm_content=thumbnail" decoding="async" loading="lazy" srcset="//thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9d/Oshkosh_Defense_%286x6%29_M-ATV_at_AUSA_2015.JPG/500px-Oshkosh_Defense_%286x6%29_M-ATV_at_AUSA_2015.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&amp;utm_campaign=parser&amp;utm_content=thumbnail 2x" data-file-width="1200" data-file-height="900" data-file-type="bitmap" height="188" width="250" class="mw-file-element" id="mwAdU"/></a><figcaption id="mwAdY">Oshkosh Defense 6×6 M-ATV at AUSA 2015</figcaption></figure>

<p id="mwAdc">In October 2015, Oshkosh unveiled an M-ATV <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Six-wheel_drive" title="Six-wheel drive" id="mwAdg">6×6-wheeled technology</a> demonstrator. The vehicle was designed with greater interior volume to transport three crew and 8–12 troops and to have greater payload capacity while maintaining MRAP-level protection and off-road mobility, combining the TAK-4 independent suspension and the M-ATV 6×6's all-wheel steer for maneuverability across any terrain. Top speed is <span about="#mwt188" typeof="mw:Transclusion" id="mwAdk" data-mw='{"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"convert","href":"./Template:Convert"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"65"},"2":{"wt":"mph"},"3":{"wt":"km/h"},"abbr":{"wt":"on"}},"i":0}}]}'>65</span><span typeof="mw:Entity" about="#mwt188"> </span><span about="#mwt188">mph (105</span><span typeof="mw:Entity" about="#mwt188"> </span><span about="#mwt188">km/h)</span> with a 70 percent off-road/30 percent on-road suspension durability profile. It has a <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Curb_weight" title="Curb weight" class="mw-redirect" id="mwAdo">curb weight</a> of 21 tons (<span about="#mwt189" typeof="mw:Transclusion" id="mwAds" data-mw='{"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"convert","href":"./Template:Convert"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"42,000"},"2":{"wt":"lb"},"3":{"wt":"kg"},"abbr":{"wt":"on"},"disp":{"wt":"or"}},"i":0}}]}'>42,000</span><span typeof="mw:Entity" about="#mwt189"> </span><span about="#mwt189">lb or 19,000</span><span typeof="mw:Entity" about="#mwt189"> </span><span about="#mwt189">kg</span>), a payload capacity of <span about="#mwt190" typeof="mw:Transclusion" id="mwAdw" data-mw='{"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"convert","href":"./Template:Convert"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"12,000"},"2":{"wt":"lb"},"3":{"wt":"kg"},"abbr":{"wt":"on"}},"i":0}}]}'>12,000</span><span typeof="mw:Entity" about="#mwt190"> </span><span about="#mwt190">lb (5,400</span><span typeof="mw:Entity" about="#mwt190"> </span><span about="#mwt190">kg)</span>, and the same turning radius as the 4x4 version.<sup about="#mwt191" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-56" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-56"}}'><a href="#cite_note-56" id="mwAd0"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwAd4"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAd8">[</span>56<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAeA">]</span></span></a></sup><sup about="#mwt194" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-57" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-57"}}'><a href="#cite_note-57" id="mwAeE"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwAeI"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAeM">[</span>57<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAeQ">]</span></span></a></sup></p>

